WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.926 line:70% position:50% size:90%
How can a robot adapt to terrain while preserving the intended
behavior?

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.516 line:70% position:50% size:90%
P-BFM separates intent from execution.

00:00:10.516 --> 00:00:17.555 line:70% position:50% size:90%
It learns from forty flat-ground LaFAN motion sequences and
interaction with simulated terrains.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:26.905 line:96% position:50% size:90%
In simulation, the same reference motion guides behavior
across stairs, platforms, box obstacles, and slopes.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:35.932 line:70% position:50% size:90%
On the real robot, live human motion supplies the intent for
teleoperation.

00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:39.418 line:70% position:50% size:90%
The robot adapts its movements to the surrounding terrain.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.529 line:70% position:50% size:90%
Rewards for forward speed and pelvis height specify the
behavioral intent.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.853 line:70% position:50% size:90%
For motion tracking, a reference motion supplies the intent.

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:06.432 line:70% position:50% size:90%
Onboard depth perception guides whole-body execution over
uneven terrain.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.800 line:70% position:50% size:90%
Explicit heading and velocity commands help maintain direction
and forward progress.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:25.616 line:70% position:50% size:90%
For goal reaching, a target body pose specifies the intent,
without requiring a full motion trajectory.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:43.942 line:70% position:50% size:90%
When pushed or kicked, the robot adjusts its posture and foot
placement to regain balance and continue the commanded
behavior.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.703 line:70% position:50% size:90%
One frozen policy, from simulation to the real world.

